steps.body:
You already know the multi-key pattern from sorting structs: compare the main key first, and compare the next key only when the main keys are equal. Each key can go its own direction:

```go
sort.Slice(ps, func(i, j int) bool {
	if ps[i].Score != ps[j].Score {
		return ps[i].Score > ps[j].Score
	}
	return ps[i].Name < ps[j].Name
})
```

You need that tie-breaker because `sort.Slice` is **not stable**: elements that compare equal can come out in any order, and that order can change when the data changes.

Sometimes the input order is the tie-breaker you want, such as tasks in the order they were added. No field records that order, so there is nothing to compare. `sort.SliceStable` has the same signature but keeps equal elements in their original order:

```go
sort.SliceStable(tasks, func(i, j int) bool {
	return tasks[i].Priority < tasks[j].Priority
})
```

Both sort in place by swapping elements of the slice's backing array, so every slice sharing that array sees the new order. Stable sorting does a bit more work, so use it only when the original order matters.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type Player struct {
	Name  string
	Score int
}

type Task struct {
	Name     string
	Priority int
}

func main() {
	players := []Player{{"Mia", 90}, {"Ben", 75}, {"Ava", 90}, {"Leo", 82}, {"Zoe", 75}}
	sort.Slice(players, func(i, j int) bool {
		if players[i].Score != players[j].Score {
			return players[i].Score > players[j].Score // main key: high score first
		}
		return players[i].Name < players[j].Name // tie-breaker: name A to Z
	})
	for _, p := range players {
		fmt.Printf("%-4s %d\n", p.Name, p.Score)
	}

	tasks := []Task{{"email", 2}, {"deploy", 1}, {"lunch", 3}, {"review", 1}, {"backup", 2}}
	sort.SliceStable(tasks, func(i, j int) bool {
		return tasks[i].Priority < tasks[j].Priority
	})
	for _, t := range tasks {
		fmt.Println(t.Priority, t.Name)
	}
}

